1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
Two beakers of tap water look exactly the

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:03,120
same.

3
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:06,160
One holds lead above the drinking water guideline,

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:08,160
just a few millionths of a gram in

5
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:08,960
every litre.

6
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,920
You can't see that, and no precipitate test

7
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:13,040
would show so little.

8
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,560
Now a copper solution: the deeper the blue,

9
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:17,520
the more copper.

10
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,800
Both are questions about how much, and light

11
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:21,920
can answer both.

12
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,680
So how do colour and absorption measure concentration?

13
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:29,460
You met the colourimeter in Module 5.

14
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,580
Light passes through a cuvette, the solution absorbs

15
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:35,300
some of it, and the meter reports absorbance.

16
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:38,820
A blank sets zero, and absorbance rises in

17
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:40,540
step with concentration.

18
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:42,660
So which light should you use?

19
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:45,940
A copper solution looks blue because it absorbs

20
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:48,100
orange and red light and lets the blue

21
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:48,660
through.

22
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:51,380
So you measure with the colour it absorbs,

23
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:52,740
not the colour you see.

24
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:55,780
In a colourimeter, that's a red or orange

25
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:56,500
filter.

26
00:00:57,700 --> 00:00:59,700
A filter lets through a whole band of

27
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:00,900
colours, though.

28
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:03,860
A UV-visible spectrophotometer does better.

29
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:06,980
Inside, a grating spreads the light into a

30
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,860
rainbow, and a narrow slit picks out one

31
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:10,660
wavelength.

32
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,260
It reaches into ultraviolet too, so some colourless

33
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:15,780
substances can be measured.

34
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,960
Step through the wavelengths, measuring at each, and

35
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:21,720
you get an absorption spectrum.

36
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:24,840
For the deep blue copper ammonia complex, the

37
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:27,720
peak sits near six hundred nanometres, in the

38
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:28,520
orange.

39
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:31,800
Measure there, at the peak, called lambda max.

40
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,400
Absorbance changes most with concentration at the peak,

41
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,720
and the top is fairly flat, so a

42
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:40,840
tiny slip in wavelength barely changes the reading.

43
00:01:42,067 --> 00:01:44,506
Why use the ammonia complex at all?

44
00:01:44,867 --> 00:01:47,346
Copper ions in water are pale blue and

45
00:01:47,467 --> 00:01:49,066
absorb only weakly.

46
00:01:49,347 --> 00:01:52,946
Ammonia molecules bond around each copper ion, making

47
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:55,746
the deep blue complex from the cation video,

48
00:01:55,827 --> 00:01:58,226
and it absorbs several times more strongly.

49
00:01:58,627 --> 00:02:01,906
So even low concentrations give a clear reading.

50
00:02:02,227 --> 00:02:04,786
Add the ammonia in excess, to the standards

51
00:02:04,867 --> 00:02:07,746
and the sample alike, so every copper ion

52
00:02:07,827 --> 00:02:09,266
ends up in the complex.

53
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:13,906
The law behind this is Beer-Lambert: A equals

54
00:02:14,067 --> 00:02:15,826
epsilon l c.

55
00:02:16,307 --> 00:02:18,546
Say a solution reads an absorbance of point

56
00:02:18,787 --> 00:02:19,986
four eight zero,

57
00:02:20,547 --> 00:02:22,386
epsilon is a hundred and twenty litres per

58
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:25,346
mole per centimetre, and the cuvette is one

59
00:02:25,507 --> 00:02:27,026
centimetre wide.

60
00:02:27,107 --> 00:02:31,186
Rearrange for c, absorbance over epsilon times l,

61
00:02:31,267 --> 00:02:34,226
and that's point zero zero four zero zero

62
00:02:34,387 --> 00:02:36,066
moles per litre.

63
00:02:36,147 --> 00:02:38,226
A book value of epsilon may not suit

64
00:02:38,307 --> 00:02:41,426
your instrument's light, though, so chemists usually trust

65
00:02:41,507 --> 00:02:44,066
a calibration line made on their own instrument.

66
00:02:45,267 --> 00:02:48,226
Lead ions are colourless, and far too dilute

67
00:02:48,347 --> 00:02:50,066
for any colour test.

68
00:02:50,147 --> 00:02:53,026
So for metals at trace levels, chemists use

69
00:02:53,267 --> 00:02:57,026
atomic absorption spectroscopy, or AAS.

70
00:02:57,347 --> 00:02:59,426
The sample is sprayed into a flame.

71
00:02:59,907 --> 00:03:02,386
The water evaporates, and the heat breaks the

72
00:03:02,467 --> 00:03:05,346
lead compounds apart into free lead atoms.

73
00:03:05,747 --> 00:03:07,426
That's called atomising.

74
00:03:07,747 --> 00:03:10,226
A hollow cathode lamp, with its cathode made

75
00:03:10,307 --> 00:03:13,346
of lead, shines lead's own wavelengths through the

76
00:03:13,427 --> 00:03:13,906
flame.

77
00:03:14,547 --> 00:03:17,426
Lead atoms absorb those wavelengths as their electrons

78
00:03:17,507 --> 00:03:20,306
jump to higher energy levels, and the detector

79
00:03:20,467 --> 00:03:22,466
measures how much light went missing.

80
00:03:23,667 --> 00:03:26,386
Other atoms in the flame need different energies,

81
00:03:26,467 --> 00:03:28,626
so they let lead's light pass.

82
00:03:29,267 --> 00:03:31,746
In a flame test, heated atoms give out

83
00:03:31,867 --> 00:03:32,866
their own colours.

84
00:03:33,427 --> 00:03:36,466
In AAS, atoms in the flame take in

85
00:03:36,587 --> 00:03:38,386
those same wavelengths from the lamp.

86
00:03:38,947 --> 00:03:42,146
So AAS measures atoms in the flame, not

87
00:03:42,307 --> 00:03:43,746
ions in the solution.

88
00:03:43,987 --> 00:03:45,506
That makes it specific.

89
00:03:45,827 --> 00:03:48,066
For the lowest levels, like lead in drinking

90
00:03:48,147 --> 00:03:51,586
water, a heated graphite tube replaces the flame.

91
00:03:52,067 --> 00:03:55,106
The costs: one lamp means one element at

92
00:03:55,147 --> 00:03:57,986
a time, and other substances in the sample

93
00:03:58,067 --> 00:04:01,426
can change how many atoms form, so standards

94
00:04:01,587 --> 00:04:03,106
should resemble the sample.

95
00:04:04,334 --> 00:04:09,213
Like the colourimeter, AAS gives absorbance, not concentration,

96
00:04:09,294 --> 00:04:11,133
so it needs a calibration.

97
00:04:11,454 --> 00:04:13,773
Standards first, unknown second.

98
00:04:14,254 --> 00:04:16,653
Run a blank, then lead standards from point

99
00:04:16,734 --> 00:04:19,373
five to two milligrams per litre, and plot

100
00:04:19,494 --> 00:04:21,573
absorbance against concentration.

101
00:04:21,854 --> 00:04:24,173
The points fall close to a straight line.

102
00:04:24,494 --> 00:04:26,893
Now an unknown can be read across, then

103
00:04:26,974 --> 00:04:29,533
down, as long as its reading sits inside

104
00:04:29,574 --> 00:04:30,413
the standards.

105
00:04:31,634 --> 00:04:34,433
Here's a factory discharge tested for lead.

106
00:04:34,554 --> 00:04:37,193
Its first reading is above the top standard.

107
00:04:37,474 --> 00:04:39,793
Don't stretch the line past the last point,

108
00:04:39,954 --> 00:04:42,753
because nothing shows it stays straight out there.

109
00:04:42,794 --> 00:04:45,953
Dilute it instead: twenty five millilitres made up

110
00:04:46,034 --> 00:04:49,393
to two hundred and fifty, a tenfold dilution.

111
00:04:49,514 --> 00:04:52,113
Now it reads point one one three, inside

112
00:04:52,154 --> 00:04:52,833
the range.

113
00:04:54,034 --> 00:04:54,833
Now read it.

114
00:04:55,234 --> 00:04:57,793
The line's gradient is point one six two

115
00:04:57,874 --> 00:05:02,193
absorbance units per milligram per litre, so divide:

116
00:05:02,314 --> 00:05:04,753
point one one three over point one six

117
00:05:04,914 --> 00:05:07,873
two gives point six nine eight milligrams per

118
00:05:07,954 --> 00:05:08,353
litre.

119
00:05:08,914 --> 00:05:10,513
But that's the diluted solution.

120
00:05:10,994 --> 00:05:14,193
Multiply back by the dilution factor, ten, and

121
00:05:14,234 --> 00:05:17,073
the discharge holds six point nine eight milligrams

122
00:05:17,154 --> 00:05:18,353
of lead per litre.

123
00:05:19,567 --> 00:05:20,766
So which method?

124
00:05:21,247 --> 00:05:23,406
If the species is coloured, or can be

125
00:05:23,487 --> 00:05:26,606
made coloured with a complex, colourimetry is quick

126
00:05:26,767 --> 00:05:30,286
and cheap, and UV-visible is more selective, with

127
00:05:30,367 --> 00:05:31,926
one chosen wavelength.

128
00:05:32,207 --> 00:05:34,526
For one metal at trace levels, coloured or

129
00:05:34,607 --> 00:05:37,006
not, choose AAS.

130
00:05:38,234 --> 00:05:39,433
Two lines to keep.

131
00:05:39,914 --> 00:05:43,913
Read across, then down, only inside the standards,

132
00:05:43,994 --> 00:05:46,473
then multiply back by the dilution factor.

133
00:05:46,874 --> 00:05:49,273
And in AAS, the lamp is made of

134
00:05:49,354 --> 00:05:50,793
the element you're testing.

135
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:52,640
Your turn.

136
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:57,280
Copper in vineyard runoff is measured by AAS.

137
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:00,640
Ten millilitres was diluted to one hundred, and

138
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:04,160
the diluted solution reads point two two six.

139
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:07,200
Use the calibration to find the copper concentration

140
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:08,840
in the original runoff.

141
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:10,560
Pause here to answer.

142
00:06:20,967 --> 00:06:23,246
Read point two two six across to the

143
00:06:23,287 --> 00:06:26,646
line and down: about two point five milligrams

144
00:06:26,727 --> 00:06:27,366
per litre.

145
00:06:27,847 --> 00:06:29,646
That's the diluted solution.

146
00:06:29,927 --> 00:06:33,046
The runoff was diluted tenfold, so multiply by

147
00:06:33,207 --> 00:06:36,566
ten: about twenty five milligrams per litre.

148
00:06:36,887 --> 00:06:39,126
Stopping at two point five is the classic

149
00:06:39,207 --> 00:06:41,766
slip, because that's the diluted value.

150
00:06:42,967 --> 00:06:43,606
Last one.

151
00:06:43,847 --> 00:06:48,086
Two samples: a deep blue copper ammonia solution,

152
00:06:48,207 --> 00:06:50,326
and tap water to be checked for traces

153
00:06:50,367 --> 00:06:50,886
of lead.

154
00:06:51,447 --> 00:06:53,686
Choose a technique for each, and justify your

155
00:06:53,767 --> 00:06:54,246
choice.

156
00:06:54,727 --> 00:06:56,006
Pause here to answer.

157
00:07:06,434 --> 00:07:11,113
For the copper complex, colourimetry or UV-visible: it's

158
00:07:11,154 --> 00:07:14,593
strongly coloured, so it absorbs visible light, best

159
00:07:14,674 --> 00:07:16,433
measured at lambda max.

160
00:07:16,754 --> 00:07:21,793
For trace lead, graphite-furnace AAS: lead is colourless,

161
00:07:21,834 --> 00:07:24,033
and the furnace measures far lower levels than

162
00:07:24,114 --> 00:07:25,153
a flame.

163
00:07:25,194 --> 00:07:27,793
Saying AAS detects the lead ions in the

164
00:07:27,874 --> 00:07:29,473
water is the trap.

165
00:07:29,554 --> 00:07:31,313
It measures free atoms.

166
00:07:32,534 --> 00:07:35,653
So, to measure concentration with light, pick a

167
00:07:35,734 --> 00:07:39,493
method your species responds to, calibrate with standards,

168
00:07:39,574 --> 00:07:41,973
read inside the range, and correct for any

169
00:07:42,054 --> 00:07:43,013
dilution.

170
00:07:43,334 --> 00:07:46,853
Next, you'll build a calibration yourself, from standards

171
00:07:46,934 --> 00:07:47,893
to an unknown.

172
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:50,540
Here are the key notes.

173
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:52,700
Pause the video if you need more time

174
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:53,580
to copy them.
